   osmotic pressure of the hemolymph, glucose levels, total count of
   hemocyte (TCH), and proPO activity in adult white shrimp Litopenaeus
   vannamei. Exposure of the shrimp to CTMax significantly increased the
   osmotic pressure of the hemolymph relative to the control group. In
   organisms reaching CTMax, temperature elicited a secondary stress
   response that included an increase in hemolymph glucose of 31mgmL(1).
   Metabolites in hemolymph such as cholesterol, acylglycerides, and total
   protein were not significantly affected by exposure to CTMax. CTMax
   exposure affected several immunological parameters causing decreases in
   TCH and proPO activity. We suggested that biomarkers such as osmolality,
   glucose levels, TCH, and proPO activity could be used as sensitive
   predictors of exposure to CTMax in white shrimp.},
